{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-01",
  "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B001",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B012",
    "B011"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration has changed from B012 to B001. The discussion sharpens the distinction between a framework with broad but indirect support and a discovery whose physical principle, implementation, reproducibility, and present scientific use are all exceptionally clear. The magic-wavelength lattice permits confinement without the ordinarily destructive trapping shift, creating a distinct regime of precision measurement rather than simply another incremental clock improvement. Katori's foundational concept and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact and intelligible attribution. The achievement has been independently sustained and has already enabled fundamental measurements; it does not rest on a promised future application.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains the central reservation. Optical-frequency metrology has a long prehistory, and other laboratories have made important contributions to performance, comparisons, and applications. The award must therefore be confined to the specific invention and development of optical lattice clocks, not be presented as credit for optical clocks generally or for all subsequent record-setting work.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that B001 is a mature experimental achievement because the magic-wavelength principle has been independently reproduced, supports established precision measurements, and permits a complementary Katori-Ye attribution.",
        "response": "I find this comparison decisive. My earlier preference for B012 reflected the extraordinary explanatory reach of inflation, but Kroll correctly separates demonstrated scientific maturity from anticipated consequences. B001 has a direct experimental chain from principle to reproducible capability and already produces new measurements. His warning not to rely on a future redefinition of the second also usefully keeps the case focused on physics rather than institutional adoption."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that B001 is elegant quantum-state control with durable applications to clock comparisons, tests of relativity, and fundamental physics, while requiring a narrow historical account.",
        "response": "I agree with both sides of this assessment. The ability to interrogate many confined atoms while preserving the transition being measured is a genuine physical invention, not merely superior engineering. At the same time, her insistence on separating the lattice-clock breakthrough from earlier frequency metrology answers the chief attribution concern: a narrow citation can recognize the new principle and its defining realization without absorbing the entire lineage."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce regards IceCube's diffuse astrophysical flux as a durable opening of high-energy neutrino astronomy, but judges that Francis Halzen alone may not fairly represent the indispensable collaboration.",
        "response": "I retain B011 as a serious alternative because opening a new observational channel is among the most compelling kinds of discovery. Yet the attribution problem is not cosmetic: on the supplied record, the observatory's collective achievement cannot be securely reduced to one name. B001 has comparably mature experimental substance while connecting its two credited people more directly to a delimited conceptual and experimental advance."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a full priority review confirms that Katori and Ye alone are the complete and fair attribution for the specific lattice-clock breakthrough.",
      "Whether the citation can be kept sufficiently narrow to distinguish optical lattice clocks from earlier optical-frequency metrology and later performance advances.",
      "Whether future evidence could raise the absence of a distinctive direct signature for B012 from a reservation to a decisive obstacle."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile. I have no disclosed collaboration or institutional connection to the proposed B001 laureates."
  }
}
